DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20200506
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Maine Financial Institutions Superintendent w
 arns consumers of financial scams related to the anticipated direct paymen
 ts from the U.S. Government under the CARES Act
DESCRIPTION:Maine Financial Institutions Superintendent warns consumers of
  financial scams related to the anticipated direct payments from the U.S. 
 Government under the CARES Act\nMay 6\, 2020\nGARDINER – Superintenden
 t of the Maine Bureau of Financial Institutions Lloyd LaFountain is warnin
 g consumers of an expected surge in scam attempts involving the anticipate
 d direct payment to individuals under the federal Coronavirus Aid\, Relief
 \, and Economic Security Act or the CARES Act. The new law\, enacted by Co
 ngress on March 27\, 2020\, is intended to provide financial relief to all
  Americans to help alleviate the negative economic impact of the coronavir
 us pandemic. Individuals can expect to receive a direct payment from the f
 ederal government in amounts up to $1\,200 beginning in the next few weeks
 .\n“Unfortunately\, scammers will very likely try to exploit the progra
 m to engage in ID theft and to defraud innocent people of their payments. 
 Everyone is reminded to remain vigilant during the current crisis and be w
 ary of any requests for personal information\,” remarked LaFountain.\nT
 he Treasury Department and the Internal Revenue Service have announced tha
 t distribution of economic impact payments will begin in the next three we
 eks and will be distributed automatically\, with no action required for mo
 st people. The IRS indicates that Social Security beneficiaries who are no
 t typically required to file tax returns will not need to file to receive 
 a payment. Instead\, payments will be automatically deposited into their b
 ank accounts. However\, some people who typically do not file returns will
  need to submit a simple tax return to receive the economic impact payment
 . Details on how to obtain the payments will be posted on the IRS website.
  The Superintendent urges Mainers to go to\nwww.IRS.gov\nfor their informa
 tion.\nConsumers should be on the lookout for mailings\, emails\, phone ca
 lls\, and other communications from fraudsters claiming to provide consume
 rs quick access to economic impact payments. Y\nhttps://www.maine.gov/pfr/
 financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-19-maine-financial-institutions
 -superintendent-warns-consumers-financial-scams
URL:https://www.maine.gov/pfr/financialinstitutions/news-notices/2022-05-1
 9-maine-financial-institutions-superintendent-warns-consumers-financial-sc
 ams
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:246a83e563983ef7@role-tax.maine-government-feeds
DTSTAMP:20260821T015602Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260820
SUMMARY:[Curated Role Feeds] Upcoming Federal Changes to MaineCare Eligibi
 lity for Some Non-citizens
DESCRIPTION:Maine.gov\nAgencies\n|\nOnline Services\n|\nHelp\n|\nSearch Ma
 ine.gov\nDHHS\n→\nOffice of MaineCare Services (OMS)\n→\nProvider Reso
 urces\n→\n	 \n			Upcoming Federal Changes to MaineCare Eligibility for S
 ome Non-citizens\nUpcoming Federal Changes to MaineCare Eligibility for So
 me Non-citizens\nAug 20\, 2026\nWe are writing to inform you about an upco
 ming change to MaineCare eligibility that may affect some of your MaineCar
 e patients. This change is required by federal legislation signed into law
  in July 2025 (also called H.R.1\, One Big Beautiful\nBill\n\, P.L.119-21\
 , or Working Families Tax Cuts Act) that made changes to the Medicaid prog
 ram nationally. These changes apply in all states\, including Maine.\nBegi
 nning October 1\, 2026\, MaineCare eligibility is changing for some noncit
 izen groups. As a result\,\nsome individuals\nwho currently receive full M
 aineCare benefits will no longer qualify for full coverage and will instea
 d be eligible only for Emergency Services MaineCare. An explanation of cov
 ered benefits under Emergency Services MaineCare is available in the\nMain
 eCare Benefits Manual\n\, Chapter 1\, Section 1.02-4(D).\nThese changes wi
 ll\nnot\naffect children under age 21\, or pregnant individuals through 12
  months after the end of their pregnancy. Please note that the Office for 
 Family Independence (OFI) is still completing the state rulemaking process
  to implement these federal requirements.\nWhat You Should Know:\nThe elig
 ibility changes are expected to take effect on October 1\, 2026.\nMembers 
 who are affected will receive a formal Notice of Decision from OFI before 
 any eligibility changes take place. These notices are expected to be maile
 d in September 2026.\nSome members received an early awareness letter in J
 une or July 2026 to let them know they may be affected. This letter was in
 formational only and is not an eligibility determination.\nHow You Can Hel
 p:\nAs a trusted healthcare provider\, you can play an important role in h
 elping your patients prepare for these changes by:\nEncouraging pati\nhttp
 s://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/upcoming-federal-c
 hanges-mainecare-eligibility-some-non-citizens-2026-08-20
URL:https://www.maine.gov/dhhs/oms/providers/provider-bulletins/upcoming-f
 ederal-changes-mainecare-eligibility-some-non-citizens-2026-08-20
CATEGORIES:Curated Role Feeds
TRANSP:TRANSPARENT
END:VEVENT
